1
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:06,800
A home robot is not one program. It is a crowd

2
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:10,800
of services on one small computer, all at once. A camera

3
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:14,800
makes depth at thirty hertz. People perception runs near ten. Mapping,

4
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:18,800
voice, and a control loop at fifty hertz on isolated cores.

5
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:22,800
They share one C P U and one G P U.

6
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:26,387
The benchmark answers two questions for this stack on this computer.

7
00:00:26,387 --> 00:00:29,973
Does it all fit at once? And when something gives, what

8
00:00:29,973 --> 00:00:33,560
runs out first — the C P U, the G P

9
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:37,147
U, or memory? A single average utilisation number cannot tell you

10
00:00:37,147 --> 00:00:40,733
whether the robot still walks while it thinks.

11
00:00:40,733 --> 00:00:44,376
Predict before you measure. Write a budget ledger: time equals arithmetic

12
00:00:44,376 --> 00:00:48,019
over the compute rate, plus bytes over the bandwidth, plus C

13
00:00:48,019 --> 00:00:51,662
P U overhead. A prediction made before the data cannot be

14
00:00:51,662 --> 00:00:55,305
tuned to fit it. And it warns you: a small batch-one

15
00:00:55,305 --> 00:00:58,948
model does little math per launch. Like a van stuck at

16
00:00:58,948 --> 00:01:02,590
every traffic light, it spends its time between kernels, not computing.

17
00:01:02,590 --> 00:01:06,233
It is launch-bound, far slower than its roofline.

18
00:01:06,233 --> 00:01:10,062
Measure each service alone first, at three levels. L one is the

19
00:01:10,062 --> 00:01:13,890
bare engine. L two is the service as deployed, with its real

20
00:01:13,890 --> 00:01:17,719
glue. L three adds the real upstream feeding it. The gaps are

21
00:01:17,719 --> 00:01:21,548
the finding: L two minus L one is the service's own C

22
00:01:21,548 --> 00:01:25,376
P U glue, and that glue usually costs more than the network.

23
00:01:25,376 --> 00:01:29,205
And an event-driven service with no input shows an idle floor, near

24
00:01:29,205 --> 00:01:33,033
zero, not its working cost. You must drive it.

25
00:01:33,033 --> 00:01:37,300
Now the bench. Everything that stands in for the world — fake

26
00:01:37,300 --> 00:01:41,567
motor boards, a fake microphone, cloud stubs, synthetic people — runs on

27
00:01:41,567 --> 00:01:45,833
a second machine. If the harness ran on the robot, it would

28
00:01:45,833 --> 00:01:50,100
steal the very cycles you are trying to measure. A dedicated link

29
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:54,367
carries the control bus; a separate link carries the bulk feeds.

30
00:01:54,367 --> 00:01:58,450
The bench can lie in confident ways. A shared link lets

31
00:01:58,450 --> 00:02:02,533
bulk traffic slow the control bus below its rate. Device interrupts

32
00:02:02,533 --> 00:02:06,617
all land on core zero and disturb the loop. Turning on

33
00:02:06,617 --> 00:02:10,700
tracing can add milliseconds to the very latency it measures. And

34
00:02:10,700 --> 00:02:14,783
a benchmark fed all-zero data runs faster than real data, because

35
00:02:14,783 --> 00:02:18,867
the datapath never toggles. A clean number can still be wrong.

36
00:02:18,867 --> 00:02:23,050
To stop yourself tuning a result, freeze the rules first. Warm

37
00:02:23,050 --> 00:02:27,233
up, then measure a fixed window. Before its numbers count, gates

38
00:02:27,233 --> 00:02:31,417
check the window: services alive, rates as expected, no crash, no

39
00:02:31,417 --> 00:02:35,600
stray C P U, clocks locked at both ends. Conditions run

40
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:39,783
interleaved, so slow drift cancels. Hypotheses and thresholds are pre-registered, so

41
00:02:39,783 --> 00:02:43,967
a result cannot be tuned into passing.

42
00:02:43,967 --> 00:02:47,614
Finally, run the whole stack together. The combined cost beats the

43
00:02:47,614 --> 00:02:51,262
sum of the solos, because the services fight over C P

44
00:02:51,262 --> 00:02:54,910
U, G P U, and memory. That ratio is the interference

45
00:02:54,910 --> 00:02:58,557
factor. Each timing promise is judged pass, unresolved, or fail, and

46
00:02:58,557 --> 00:03:02,205
a pre-set rule names the first resource to give. On a

47
00:03:02,205 --> 00:03:05,852
small robot computer, it is often the C P U quota

48
00:03:05,852 --> 00:03:09,500
that fills before the G P U.

